<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Apostrophes - When to use</h1>
  <div class="info-stack">
    <section class="info-card card-contraction">
      <h2>Apostrophes for Contractions (Missing Letters)</h2>
      <p>
        A contraction shortens a pair of words by removing letters. The apostrophe shows where the
        letters are missing.
      </p>
      <p>Ex: do not → don’t, it is/it has → it’s, I will → I’ll</p>
      <p class="rule-label">Trap: its vs it’s</p>
      <p><strong>it’s</strong> (it is or it has)</p>
      <p>Ex: It’s raining outside.</p>
      <p><strong>its</strong> (shows possession)</p>
      <p>The dog wagged its tail.</p>
      <p class="rule-label">
        Rule: If you can’t expand it to it is or it has, don’t use an apostrophe.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="info-card card-possession">
      <h2>Apostrophes for Possession</h2>
      <h3>Singular Possession</h3>
      <p>Add ’s to show something belongs to one person or thing.</p>
      <p>
        Ex: The girl’s book. (book of one girl), The dog’s bone. (bone of one dog), The child’s toy.
        (toy of one child)
      </p>
      <h3>Plural Possession</h3>
      <p>If a plural ends in s, add the apostrophe after the s.</p>
      <p>Ex: The girls’ bags. (bags of several girls), The dogs’ tails. (tails of several dogs)</p>
      <p>If a plural does not end in s, add ’s.</p>
      <p>Ex: The children’s playground, The men’s shoes, The people’s choice.</p>
      <h3>Shared vs Individual Possession</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Shared: one apostrophe → Ben and Tom’s room (they share one room).</li>
        <li>Separate: repeat ’s → Ben’s and Tom’s rooms (each has his own).</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
